[{"order":1,"type":"heading","text":"Hibernate Fetch Strategies: Lazy vs Eager, FetchJoin, and Subselect"},{"order":2,"type":"paragraph","text":"Understanding fetch strategies in Hibernate is crucial for building high-performance Java applications. Improper fetching can lead to the infamous N+1 select problem, memory exhaustion, or unnecessary database queries."},{"order":3,"type":"subheading","text":"1. Lazy vs Eager Fetching"},{"order":4,"type":"paragraph","text":"Hibernate provides two primary strategies for loading associated entities: FetchType.LAZY and FetchType.EAGER."},{"order":5,"type":"bullet_point","text":"LAZY (Default for collections): Defays the loading of the associated data until it is explicitly accessed in the code. This is generally recommended for performance."},{"order":6,"type":"bullet_point","text":"EAGER (Default for to-one associations): Loads the associated data immediately along with the parent entity using a JOIN query. This can cause performance degradation if overused."},{"order":7,"type":"code","text":"@Entity
public class User {
    @Id
    @GeneratedValue(strategy = GenerationType.IDENTITY)
    private Long id;

    private String username;

    // Lazy fetching for collections
    @OneToMany(mappedBy = "user", fetch = FetchType.LAZY)
    private List<Order> orders;
}"},{"order":8,"type":"subheading","text":"2. Solving the N+1 Problem with FetchJoin"},{"order":9,"type":"paragraph","text":"When using lazy loading, iterating over a collection of parent entities and accessing their children triggers a separate query for each parent (1 initial query + N queries for children). A JPQL Fetch Join resolves this by retrieving parent and child data in a single SQL query."},{"order":10,"type":"code","text":"@Query("SELECT u FROM User u JOIN FETCH u.orders WHERE u.id = :id")
User findUserWithOrders(@Param("id") Long id);"},{"order":11,"type":"subheading","text":"3. Batch and Subselect Fetching"},{"order":12,"type":"paragraph","text":"If you cannot use a fetch join, Hibernate provides alternative batching mechanisms to mitigate the N+1 problem. The @Subselect annotation allows you to load associated collections for all previously loaded entities using a single secondary query."},{"order":13,"type":"code","text":"@Entity
public class User {
    @Id
    private Long id;

    @OneToMany(mappedBy = "user")
    @Subselect("SELECT * FROM orders WHERE user_id IN (SELECT id FROM users)")
    private List<Order> orders;
}"},{"order":14,"type":"subheading","text":"Summary Best Practices"},{"order":15,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Always default your relationships to FetchType.LAZY."},{"order":16,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Use JPQL JOIN FETCH explicitly in your queries when you know you will need the associated data immediately."},{"order":17,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Utilize @BatchSize or @Subselect to optimize lazy collection initialization when fetching multiple parent entities."}]
